[32m+[m[32m    "([return to the outline](#Navigating-the-Project))\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "Now that we have all of our information about each student in one DataFrame, we can compute their z-score on both the original midterm exam and the redemption questions on the final exam.\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "#### `z_score`\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "Complete the implementation of the function `z_score`, which takes in a Series of numbers and returns a Series in which all elements are converted to z-scores. As a reminder, to convert a sequence of numbers to z-scores, or standard units, we use the following formula:\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "$$z(x_i) = \\frac{x_i - \\text{mean of } x}{\\text{SD of }x}$$\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "***Notes***:\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "- Make sure to set the `ddof=0` in whichever method or function you use to compute standard deviation. `numpy` and `pandas` both use different default denominators when computing standard deviation. (`ddof=0` computes the \"population\" standard deviation and `ddof=1` computes the \"sample\" standard deviation.)\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "- Do **not** fill null values – that is, if a value in the input Series is `NaN`, its value in the output Series should also be `NaN`. (Depending on how you implement `z_score`, this may happen automatically.)\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "    - We will address null midterm scores in `add_post_redemption` below, not here in `z_score`.\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "<br>\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "#### `add_post_redemption`\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "Complete the implementation of the function `add_post_redemption`, which takes in a DataFrame like `grades_combined` and returns a DataFrame with all the columns from `grades_combined` in addition to two new columns:\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "- `'Midterm Score Pre-Redemption'`, which contains each student's midterm exam score as a proportion between 0 and 1 **before** redemption.\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "- `'Midterm Score Post-Redemption'`, which containing each student's midterm exam score **after** the redemption policy has been applied, again as a proportion between 0 and 1.\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "You can use your `z_score` function to compute the z-scores of each student's original midterm exam grades and raw redemption scores. **Note that there are students who didn't take the midterm; such students need to have their `NaN` scores fixed prior to calculating their pre-redemption z-scores**, otherwise, you may end up incorrectly giving them `NaN` post-redemption midterm scores. None of the redemption z-scores should be `NaN`, since you handled null values in your implementation of `raw_redemption`.\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "\n",[m
[32m+[m[32m    "If it's not clear, **computing the `'Midterm Score Post-Redemption'` column is the most complicated part of this question**. Make sure you understand how the redemption policy for DSC 67 works before approaching this question. If you need to refresh your understanding, re-read the instructions at the start of [Part 2](#part2)."[m
